## GFG — First Negative Integer in Every Window of Size K

### Problem
Given an array and window size k, find the first negative integer in every window of size k. If no negative integer exists in a window, output 0.

### Constraints
- `1 <= arr.length <= 10⁶`
- `-10⁵ <= arr[i] <= 10⁵`
- `1 <= k <= arr.length`

### Edge Cases
- No negative in any window → all outputs are 0
- All negatives → first element of each window is the answer
- k=1 → positives become 0, negatives stay as is

---

### Approach 1 — Brute Force

For every window of size k, scan all k elements and pick the first negative. If none found, add 0. Time is O(n×k) and auxiliary space is O(1).

```java
static List<Integer> firstNegInt(int arr[], int k) {
    int n = arr.length;
    List<Integer> list = new ArrayList<>();
    for(int i = 0; i <= n-k; i++) {
        int count = 0;
        for(int j = i; j < i+k; j++) {
            if(arr[j] < 0 && count == 0) {
                list.add(arr[j]);
                count++;
            }
        }
        if(count == 0)
            list.add(0);
    }
    return list;
}
```

---

### Approach 2 — Deque, Two Loops

First loop builds the initial window tracking only negative indices in the Deque. Second loop slides the window, removes expired front indices, adds new negatives, and records the first negative of each window. Time is O(n) and auxiliary space is O(k).

The key insight is that we only store indices of negative elements in the Deque — so the front always points to the first negative in the current window. If Deque is empty, no negative exists in that window and we output 0.

```java
static List<Integer> firstNegInt(int arr[], int k) {
    int n = arr.length;
    List<Integer> list = new ArrayList<>();
    Deque<Integer> dq = new ArrayDeque<>();

    // First loop — build initial window
    for(int i = 0; i < k; i++) {
        if(arr[i] < 0)
            dq.addLast(i);
    }

    if(!dq.isEmpty())
        list.add(arr[dq.peekFirst()]);
    else
        list.add(0);

    // Second loop — slide and record
    for(int i = k; i < n; i++) {
        if(!dq.isEmpty() && dq.peekFirst() < i-k+1)
            dq.pollFirst();
        if(arr[i] < 0)
            dq.addLast(i);
        if(dq.isEmpty())
            list.add(0);
        else
            list.add(arr[dq.peekFirst()]);
    }
    return list;
}
```

