### Approach 3 — Deque, Single Loop

Same logic as Approach 2 but combined into one loop with `i >= k-1` check to start recording. Cleaner and more compact. Time is O(n) and auxiliary space is O(k).

```java
static List<Integer> firstNegInt(int arr[], int k) {
    int n = arr.length;
    List<Integer> list = new ArrayList<>();
    Deque<Integer> dq = new ArrayDeque<>();

    for(int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        if(!dq.isEmpty() && dq.peekFirst() < i-k+1)
            dq.pollFirst();
        if(arr[i] < 0)
            dq.addLast(i);
        if(i >= k-1) {
            if(dq.isEmpty())
                list.add(0);
            else
                list.add(arr[dq.peekFirst()]);
        }
    }
    return list;
}
```

---

### Overall Comparison

Brute force at O(n×k) is straightforward but too slow for large inputs. Deque approach achieves O(n) because each element is added and removed from the Deque at most once across the entire traversal — giving O(2n) which simplifies to O(n). The single loop variant is preferred for its compactness while maintaining the same complexity.
